PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F311A1786475919
OL,3,CWhy is looking directly at a solar
OL,4,Ceclipse dangerous?
OL,6,FImo Bell, a planetarium astronomer at
OL,7,Fthe observatory, said: "It's not a
OL,8,Ftotal eclipse, but over 90% is really
OL,9,Fexciting. We're not going to get one
OL,10,Fthis good for a really long time."
OL,12,FFollowing the 1999 total solar
OL,13,Feclipse, the Royal College of
OL,14,FOphthalmologists reported around 70
OL,15,Fcases of people experiencing vision
OL,16,Fproblems after watching it. Around 40%
OL,17,Fhad looked at it for less than a
OL,18,Fminute.
OL,20,FBut why is it dangerous, and how can
OL,21,Fyou view it safely? ITV News explains.
OL,26,@oD@XOU
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G2/11
